                                                                                "summary":  "Damodaran examines the 2026 rise in the 10-year Treasury toward 4.75% and argues the increase is driven by fundamental economic forces -- real growth expectations and roughly 2.5% embedded inflation -- rather than Fed policy alone. He demonstrates that higher rates have not crushed equities because corporate earnings growth has largely offset the mechanical drag on valuations from higher discount rates. The impact is uneven across sectors: energy and large-cap tech held up while consumer-facing businesses struggled, and international markets diverged based on local rates and currency dynamics. He concludes the rates-vs-stocks relationship is too context-dependent to support simple rules like \u0027higher rates always mean lower P/Es.\u0027",
                                                                                "take":  "Damodaran\u0027s core point is the one most market commentators skip: it is the source of rate moves, not just the level, that determines equity impact. Rates rising because growth is durable is a very different animal than rates rising because inflation is unanchored. With the 10-year at 4.75% but earnings still compounding, the bears invoking the discount-rate math alone are fighting the wrong battle. The actionable read: sector rotation matters more than index-level rate sensitivity right now -- favor businesses with pricing power and stable free cash flow over rate-sensitive long-duration stories.",
