                                                                                "author":  "Aswath Damodaran",
                                                                                "url":  "https://aswathdamodaran.blogspot.com/2026/09/the-scaling-and-profitability-trade-off.html",
                                                                                "date":  "2026-09-02",
                                                                                "imageUrl":  "https://blogger.googleusercontent.com/img/b/R29vZ2xl/AVvXsEgh5fXUk3rKQgi_I28N1acK-5yXvwUPErlVboSe6pyCHZL8x0-r5qMMHlHdVhSxhBxFeuLGARgSFuwvCTDO_AdIgPsSjnpLeGDHHWmsXuT9hy7GID8Rkfe5QL_54HQTfOG1JUheL-vNqI2rSUlHl79wOqBiuwCGhvwRaQkpP2pQrPQ_PantALROtViRqHw/s320/Khoslatweet.jpg",
                                                                                "summary":  "Damodaran argues that VC\u0027s cultural obsession with scaling revenue ahead of profitability has become a structural flaw, one amplified by the fact that late-stage private capital and public markets now fund growth-stage companies that historically would have been forced to discipline themselves earlier. Companies reaching IPO are larger in revenue than prior generations but meaningfully less profitable, suggesting the growth-at-all-costs playbook often papers over broken unit economics rather than fixing them. The incentive misalignment is systemic: VCs and founders are rewarded for exits achieved through scale, not for sustainable margin structures. Damodaran concludes that for many startups, \u0027scale first, profit later\u0027 is not a strategy -- it is a deferral of reckoning.",
                                                                                "take":  "This is a structural indictment of late-stage private valuations that has direct implications for anyone buying VC-backed IPOs or holding pre-IPO secondaries. The tell Damodaran surfaces -- companies are bigger at IPO but less profitable -- means public market investors are absorbing risks that prior generations of founders had to eliminate before listing. With AI startups consuming enormous capital under the same \u0027scale now\u0027 logic, this piece should be required reading before underwriting any AI infrastructure play at a revenue multiple. The question to ask every growth-stage pitch: what specifically changes at scale that makes the unit economics work, and when?",
                                                                                "isNew":  true
