                                                                                "summary":  "Josh Brown argues that today\u0027s AI infrastructure -- chatbots, coding assistants, early agents -- represents only the most primitive forerunners of what\u0027s actually coming, and that Wall Street\u0027s hand-wringing over capex overbuilding fundamentally misreads the demand curve. Industry insiders are unanimous that compute supply is not even close to sufficient for the agentic AI era, which requires supporting autonomous vehicles, humanoid robots, and AI agents serving at civilizational scale. Brown contends the current buildout is not a bubble overshoot but a deliberate down payment on infrastructure that will be undersized within years.",
                                                                                "take":  "Brown is making a supply-demand argument that the market keeps fumbling: the concern about overbuilding assumes demand is roughly knowable, but agentic AI demand is an order-of-magnitude step-function, not a smooth curve. If he is right, the hyperscalers\u0027 capex is rational and the multiple compression from \u0027AI spending fear\u0027 represents a mispricing. The risk to this view is timing -- even correct infrastructure bets can destroy equity value if the demand ramp is slower than the depreciation schedule. Position accordingly: the thesis favors long-duration holders and punishes anyone forced to mark-to-market quarterly.",
