                                                                                "summary":  "Josh Brown profiles Ron Baron of Baron Capital, tracing how an unwavering willingness to hold concentrated positions -- most famously early and massive stakes in Tesla and SpaceX -- through years of public ridicule produced returns that ultimately silenced critics. The fund\u0027s outperformance is rooted in Baron\u0027s conviction-over-consensus approach: buying and holding businesses he believes will be worth multiples more in a decade, regardless of short-term sentiment or analyst consensus. Brown frames the $60 billion returned figure not as luck but as the compounded result of a repeatable, if psychologically demanding, process.",
                                                                                "take":  "The lesson here cuts against the passive-investing default in a useful way: Baron\u0027s edge was not stock-picking skill in the abstract -- it was the rare ability to sit through 80% drawdowns on positions he remained fundamentally certain about. Most investors, retail and institutional alike, do not have that temperament or mandate. The practical takeaway is not \u0027find the next Tesla early\u0027 but \u0027match your holding period to your conviction level\u0027 -- the investors who rode Baron\u0027s funds to those returns also had to stay in during the ugly years. Position sizing and holding discipline are the underrated variable, not the initial security selection.",
